  "query_preview": "/Sunday through Thursday/...\nIssued 312 AM MDT THU SEP 3 2026\nThe large upper level low shifts slowly eastward directly over \nthe Pacific Northwest on Sunday. As the system pivots inland, it\nwill pull additional Pacific moisture into the region, leading \nto an uptick in precipitation coverage and shower chances late \nSunday into Monday. Guidance continues to track moisture from \nHurricane Marie northward through the Great Basin into the \nNorthern Rockies. The majority of this tropical moisture is \nexpected to track east of the forecast area as the trough axis \nsits directly overhead. However, it will remain close enough for\nsouth- central Idaho to experience heightened chances for \nheavier rainfall, with snow levels dropping low enough over the \nhighest peaks for a rain and snow mix.\n\nBelow-normal temperatures will persist through early next week\ndue to upper troughing and widespread cloud cover. Heading into\nTuesday and Wednesday, ensemble guidance suggests the low will\nexit to the east, allowing an upper level ridge over the eastern\nPacific to build inland. This pattern shift will bring drier\nconditions and a gradual warming trend back toward near-normal\nseasonal temperatures by the middle to end of next week.",
